# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is git-ignored and must never be committed.
#
# ONE FILE FOR BOTH TOOLS. Searched in this order, first hit wins:
#   1. RECONCILE_ENV_FILE, if set
#   2. beside settings.py -- how a standalone copy works
#   3. the repo root -- where AMIGO's .env lives when both are installed
#
# Where AMIGO is installed alongside, its committed config/<env>.json is
# authoritative for the two facts both tools hold: api_host and entitlement.
# The RECONCILE_* equivalents below are then an OVERRIDE, not a second
# configuration -- set them only to point this tool somewhere else.
#
# If both are set and disagree, the tool says so at the top of the screen
# rather than quietly picking one. Two tools aimed at different tenants
# produce numbers that look fine and describe different estates.
#
# These names match the parent repo where they overlap, so one set of
# credentials works for both rather than drifting into two conventions:
# MOODYS_API_KEY is the same variable the Migration Hub uses, and the host and
# entitlement are the same values as api_host/entitlement in config/*.json.

# --- Moody's IRP host -------------------------------------------------------
# No trailing slash. Same value as api_host in the parent's config/*.json.
# e.g. https://api-euw1.rms.com
RECONCILE_API_HOST=https://REPLACE_WITH_MOODYS_HOST

# --- Auth -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prefer a service principal over a named user account: a personal account
# creates an audit and offboarding problem, and this list gets pulled
# repeatedly. Never commit a filled-in value.
MOODYS_API_KEY=

# Tenant entitlement, e.g. RI-RISKMODELER. Same value as `entitlement` in the
# parent's config/*.json.
RECONCILE_ENTITLEMENT=RI-RISKMODELER

# --- Endpoints --------------------------------------------------------------
# Step 3, Data Bridge. searchDatabases, confirmed against the live tenant on
# 2026-09-19:
#     https://developer.rms.com/platform/reference/searchdatabases
#
# This replaced /platform/riskdata/v1/exposures, which was chosen from the
# Postman collection before anything had been called and is the wrong family:
# riskdata/v1 lists exposures, not databases, and carries no field holding an
# on-prem database name.
#
# Needs the Data Admin role. A 403 here with a working key on step 4 means the
# key can read archives but not databases.
RECONCILE_EXPOSURES_PATH=/platform/admindata/v1/databases

# Which field on a Data Bridge row holds the SOURCE DATABASE name.
# databaseName, confirmed live -- 993 distinct values in a 1000-row page, in
# the estate's own naming. NOT unique: those collisions are real and are
# reported as Duplicate.
# RECONCILE_BRIDGE_NAME_FIELD=databaseName

# Step 4, Data Vault. searchArchives:
#     https://developer.rms.com/platform/reference/searcharchives
# Same family as step 3 now that both are admindata/v1, and the live tenant's
# key reads both. A 403 on one but not the other would mean the Data Admin
# role covers one endpoint and not its sibling.
RECONCILE_VAULT_PATH=/platform/admindata/v1/archives

# Which field on an archive row holds the SOURCE DATABASE name.
#
# A live call on 2026-09-17 returned these top-level fields:
#
#     archiveId, archiveName, archiveType, archiveSubType, archivedAt,
#     archivedBy, createdAt, createdBy, expirationDate, groupIds, owner,
#     archiveDetails (nested)
#
# There is no top-level exposureName -- asking for it returns
# CLIENT-005 "we don't recognize the field". If a previous note here said
# otherwise, it came from a sample response and the live endpoint disagrees.
#
# archiveName is the default because it exists. It is NOT confirmed to match
# an on-prem database name: it is the archive's own label and may be
# operator-assigned. Run probe_archive_api.py to print real values and see
# which field actually corresponds to your databases -- an exposure name may
# live inside archiveDetails.
#
# Get this wrong and exact matching reports the entire estate Missing -- an
# artefact of the key, not a finding. Nested fields are flattened on load, so
# a dotted path like archiveDetails.exposureName works here, and the UI lets
# you switch after a fetch.
# RECONCILE_VAULT_NAME_FIELD=archiveName

# --- Source SQL Servers (step 1) --------------------------------------------
# The Query server button runs, against whichever you pick:
#     SELECT name FROM sys.databases WHERE database_id > 4 ORDER BY name
# (database_id 1-4 are master, tempdb, model and msdb, which are not part of
# the estate and would inflate the on-prem count.)
#
# Integrated security: the query runs as whoever is logged in to Windows, so
# there is no username or password here and none in the connection string. The
# permission question is "can this person read sys.databases on that server",
# answered by the domain rather than by a file somebody has to keep out of git.
#
# Two servers, because MS Amlin confirmed two genuinely separate estates
# (GLOSSARY.md, open question 16). Querying one and calling the result "the
# on-prem inventory" would silently halve the estate. Set either or both.
#
# Direct business  -- Data Bridge 1
RECONCILE_SQL_DIRECT_HOST=SRISKPD0101
# RI / reinsurance -- Data Bridge 2
RECONCILE_SQL_RI_HOST=SRISKPD0001

# Override if the machine has a different ODBC driver installed.
# RECONCILE_SQL_DRIVER=ODBC Driver 18 for SQL Server
# Driver 18 encrypts by default and refuses a self-signed certificate, which is
# what an internal SQL Server usually has. Set to no only if yours is properly
# certificated.
# RECONCILE_SQL_TRUST_CERT=yes

# --- Display ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Target completion date, shown in the header with the days remaining.
# ISO format, YYYY-MM-DD. Defaults to 2026-10-30.
#
# A display date the tool is told, not one it derives. It is deliberately not
# read from the project plan: those dates are a baseline committed to the
# client, and coupling the two would move one when somebody edited the other.
# RECONCILE_COMPLETION_DATE=2026-10-30
